#!/bin/sh
# lypning: pre-commit hook — stage the session's sightings, and nothing else.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The Stop hook publishes <sightings>/<session>.jsonl and
# deliberately stops there: a hook that made commits would fight the session's
# own git work. But these containers are ephemeral, and evidence that is not
# committed is evidence that is lost — the whole harness ends at a file nobody
# `git add`ed, which is the one step a session, mid-way through a PR about
# something else entirely, reliably forgets. That is not hypothetical
# arithmetic about this repository: until the .claude/ directory beside this
# file was written, nothing here was wired at all, and every session that ever
# worked in this tree captured nothing — including the sessions that spent
# their time typing python one-liners at this very corpus. The harness was
# never broken here; it was never run on itself.
#
# WHERE IT LOOKS IS NOT FIXED. $LYPNING_SIGHTINGS moves the directory, and
# `--sightings` teaches people to set it, so a hook that only ever checked
# tests/corpus/sightings would find nothing, exit 0 without a word, and lose
# precisely the sightings of the contributor who followed the documentation.
# The rule below is the rule in paths.py sightings_dir() and has to stay the
# same rule: unset or blank is the default, `~` expands, absolute is used as
# given, relative resolves against the top of the working tree.
#
# DELIBERATELY NARROW. One directory, adds only. This hook runs inside somebody
# else's commit, and a commit that quietly grew a file its author did not choose
# is a worse failure than a lost sighting: the author would have to notice to
# undo it. So it stages exactly the sightings directory and never `git add -A`,
# never `-u`, never a `git commit`, never a `git stash`, and it does not care
# what else is dirty.
#
# IT MUST NEVER FAIL A COMMIT. Every git call ends in `|| true` and every path
# ends in `exit 0`. A capture harness that can reject a commit has made itself
# more important than the work, and the first time it did so under a deadline
# somebody would delete it — correctly.
#
# ACTIVATION is per clone and opt-in: `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`.
# Git will not let a checked-in file arm itself, which is the right default, and
# nothing in .claude/ runs that command on your behalf either — a capture
# harness reconfiguring your git without being asked is exactly the kind of
# thing invariant 7 forbids. An ephemeral container is a fresh clone, so it is a
# fresh `git config`; .claude/settings.json says so where a reader will look.
#
# Environment:
#   LYPNING_CAPTURE=0     the harness is off; stage nothing (LYPNING_HARVEST=0
#                         leaves capture running but publishes no sightings, so
#                         there is nothing here for this hook to find anyway)
#   LYPNING_SIGHTINGS     where the Stop hook publishes; same resolution rule as
#                         paths.py, because a second rule is a second answer

[ "${LYPNING_CAPTURE-}" = "0" ] && exit 0

# A partial commit (`git commit -- <paths>`, `git commit -p`) runs this hook
# against a TEMPORARY index, so anything staged here either misses the commit or
# lands in the real index as a surprise the author never asked for. Either way
# the author narrowed the commit on purpose and this hook is not entitled to
# widen it. Detect it the only way a hook can — GIT_INDEX_FILE pointing
# somewhere other than the repository's own index — and stand down.
#
# The comparison is against $GIT_DIR/index, and it has to be: `rev-parse
# --git-path index` HONOURS GIT_INDEX_FILE, so during the partial commit it
# reports the temporary index and the guard compares that value against itself,
# matches, and waves through the exact case it exists to catch. Measured, not
# reasoned about: `git commit -m x -- a.txt` on git 2.43 runs this hook with
# GIT_INDEX_FILE=.git/next-index-<pid>.lock, which `--git-path index` echoes
# back verbatim. `--absolute-git-dir` does not move — it is the worktree's own
# gitdir even in a linked worktree, whose index is the one git would use here.
#
# If GIT_INDEX_FILE is set and the git dir CANNOT be located, the comparison
# cannot be made and the honest answer is "unknown". Unknown stands down as
# well: standing down wrongly costs one sighting, which the next commit picks up
# anyway, while proceeding wrongly writes into somebody's narrowed commit, which
# is the one failure this whole hook is written to avoid.
if [ -n "${GIT_INDEX_FILE-}" ]; then
  git_dir=$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null) || git_dir=""
  [ -n "$git_dir" ] || exit 0
  # GIT_INDEX_FILE arrives relative on a normal commit (.git/index) and absolute
  # on a narrowed one, so spell it absolutely before comparing. Hooks run at the
  # top of the working tree; if that ever stops being true the paths differ, the
  # guard stands down, and a sighting waits for the next commit.
  case $GIT_INDEX_FILE in
    /*) index_abs=$GIT_INDEX_FILE ;;
    *) index_abs=$PWD/$GIT_INDEX_FILE ;;
  esac
  [ "$index_abs" = "$git_dir/index" ] || exit 0
fi

# Hooks are normally run from the top of the working tree, but "normally" is not
# a guarantee worth a mis-staged path, so the pathspec is anchored rather than
# relative to whatever cwd this inherited.
top=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || top=""
[ -n "$top" ] || exit 0

# paths.py strips the value before deciding, so a value that is only whitespace
# is the default and not a directory named " ". Parameter expansion only: this
# runs on every commit in the clone, nearly all of which have nothing to stage,
# and a fork is a fork.
sightings=${LYPNING_SIGHTINGS-}
while :; do
  case $sightings in
    [[:space:]]*) sightings=${sightings#?} ;;
    *[[:space:]]) sightings=${sightings%?} ;;
    *) break ;;
  esac
done
if [ -n "${HOME-}" ]; then
  case $sightings in
    "~"|"~/"*) sightings=$HOME${sightings#\~} ;;
  esac
fi
case $sightings in
  "") dir=$top/tests/corpus/sightings ;;
  /*) dir=$sightings ;;
  *) dir=$top/$sightings ;;
esac

[ -d "$dir" ] || exit 0

# `git add` on an unchanged directory is a no-op, so this is silent on the
# overwhelming majority of commits: the export is a pure function of its inputs
# and rewrites nothing when the session typed no new python. A $LYPNING_SIGHTINGS
# pointing outside the working tree makes git refuse and say so on stderr, once
# per commit; the message is left alone rather than swallowed, because a
# repository cannot commit a file it does not contain and the alternative is
# publishing sightings that silently never ship. The commit still succeeds.
git add -- "$dir" || true

exit 0
